  "query_preview": "..A trough is currently centered over AZ visible in \nmid-level water vapor satellite imagery. Large scale lift \nassociated with this feature is bringing areas of showers and \nthunderstorms this morning, primarily across northern Gila and \nsouthern Navajo/Apache counties. A few stronger thunderstorms \nbringing small hail and heavy rainfall have been observed and will\nremain a possibility over the next few hours. Showers are \nforecast to continue through the rest of the morning while \ngradually diminishing and drifting eastward. The trough axis looks\nto move past AZ by this afternoon. Subsidence and a drier \nwesterly flow will follow in its wake, but enough moisture will \nremain for lingering isolated showers and thunderstorms during the\ndaytime today. By Saturday, a much drier and capped airmass will \ndrop precipitation chances to near zero.\n\nWe may see an active stretch of weather to begin next week. A deep \nPacific trough is forecast to dive southeastward along the coast of \nCA on Sunday-Monday, then move across our region on Tuesday. A pair \nof tropical systems (Hurricane Polo and Tropical Storm Odalys) will \nlikely enhance any moisture surge associated with the trough. There \nis potential for impactful rainfall, and the current Excessive \nRainfall Outlook from WPC puts all of Northern AZ in the Slight risk \ncategory by Monday. However plenty of uncertainty still remains \namong ensemble guidance, and will hinge on the exact timing and \ntrajectory of the features. For example, LREF clusters highlight a \nlower end precipitation scenario where the trough follows a more \nprogressive and northern trajectory, and a higher end scenario where \nthe trough is deeper and slowly moves across northern AZ.\nNevertheless, virtually all guidance has some precipitation \nreturning through the period. The current forecast has slight \nchances for shower and thunderstorm activity as early as Sunday as\na weaker shortwave propagates ahead of the deep trough, then \nwidespread precipitation chances by Monday-Tuesday. There is room \nfor the details to change, so we encourage you to check back in \nfuture forecast periods!\n\nBesides precipitation, expect breezy south-southwest winds and \ncooler temperatures early next week. Much drier conditions, warming \ntemperatures, and lighter winds are forecast to return by midweek \ninto the weekend.",
